        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "4725d510-88fa-58ba-b861-a2be0e058ed9",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Organizations: Lamplighters Music Theatre\n- Works: Candide, The Merry Widow, Die Fledermaus, Of Thee I Sing, My Fair Lady, A Little Night Music\n- People: Orva Hoskinson, Ann Pool MacNab, Leonard Bernstein, Voltaire, Samuel Krachmalnick, George Maran, Paul Hindemith, Gilbert and Sullivan\n- Topics: light opera, Broadway\n\nFacts:\n- In 1759, Voltaire wrote the novella Candide.\n- In 1952, Orva Hoskinson and Ann Pool MacNab founded Lamplighters Music Theatre.\n- Lamplighters Music Theatre specializes in light opera, particularly the works of Gilbert and Sullivan.\n- Lamplighters Music Theatre also performs works including The Merry Widow, Die Fledermaus, Of Thee I Sing, My Fair Lady, Candide, and A Little Night Music.\n- During the 1950s, Samuel Krachmalnick came to prominence as a conductor on Broadway.\n- Samuel Krachmalnick received a Tony Award nomination for serving as music director of the original Broadway production of Leonard Bernstein's Candide.\n- Candide is an operetta with music composed by Leonard Bernstein and based on Voltaire's 1759 novella of the same name.\n- George Maran attended Harvard University, where Leonard Bernstein and Paul Hindemith took notice of his voice.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "d65f5fbd-a1ef-58b6-99eb-310b7e54d6ab",
        "text": "Page: Lamplighters Music Theatre. Sentence: Founded in 1952 by Orva Hoskinson and Ann Pool MacNab, the Lamplighters specialize in light opera, particularly the works of Gilbert and Sullivan, as well as such works as \"The Merry Widow\", \"Die Fledermaus\", \"Of Thee I Sing\", \"My Fair Lady\", \"Candide\", and \"A Little Night Music\".\nPage: Candide (operetta). Sentence: Candide is an operetta with music composed by Leonard Bernstein, based on the 1759 novella of the same name by Voltaire.\nPage: Samuel Krachmalnick. Sentence: He first came to prominence as a conductor on Broadway during the 1950s, notably earning a Tony Award nomination for his work as the music director of the original production of Leonard Bernstein's \"Candide\".\nPage: George Maran. Sentence: Maran attended Harvard University where he and his voice came to the attention of people such as Leonard Bernstein and Paul Hindemith.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
